{
  "id": "analysis",
  "title": "Analysis from First Principles",
  "summary": "Real and complex analysis from limits and completeness through rigorous calculus, metric spaces, holomorphic functions and conformal mapping; measure and Lebesgue integration, L-p and Hilbert spaces, followed by L-p duality, bounded operators, Hahn–Banach, weak convergence and Banach–Steinhaus. A further twelve-lecture foundational bridge now develops adjoints, spectra, continuous and Borel functional calculi, unbounded domains, holomorphic calculus, and evolution equations. A twelve-lecture bridge now develops Fourier transforms, distributions, Sobolev spaces, weak elliptic equations, and C-zero semigroups, with preceding functional-analytic representation theorems. A further foundational sequence covers Banach/C-star algebras, weak-star compactness, analytic semigroups, boundary traces, fractional and nonlinear Sobolev estimates, fixed-point methods, energy and compactness arguments, and monotone variational existence.",
  "assumed_background": [
    "Elementary algebra, inequalities, and familiarity with fractions"
  ],
